commutation-relation-between-momentum-an.conceptual_definition:
The commutation relation between the momentum operator and the Hamiltonian operator, 
$[
\hat{\mathbf{p}},
\hat{H}
] = 0$
is a fundamental statement in quantum mechanics that signifies whether momentum and energy are simultaneously observable quantities for a given quantum system. If the commutator is zero, it implies that the momentum and the Hamiltonian of the system commute, meaning that there exists a set of simultaneous eigenstates for both operators. This, in turn, indicates that precise measurements of both momentum and energy can be made at the same time without disturbing each other. Conversely, if the commutator is non-zero, momentum and energy cannot be simultaneously known with arbitrary precision, as dictated by the Heisenberg uncertainty principle.
